<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herbert Samuel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Herbert Samuel, 1st Viscount Samuel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herbert Samuel was a British Liberal politician and the first practising Jew in a British cabinet. In July 1920 he became the first High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, replacing the military Occupied Enemy Territory Administration with a civil government. He served until 1925, during the early years of conflict between the Jewish and Arab national movements.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herbert Samuel was born in Toxteth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, in 1870. His uncle Samuel Montagu founded the bank of that name. He was educated at University College School in Hampstead and at Balliol College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he renounced all religious belief in 1892, influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>. He nonetheless remained a member of the Jewish community to please his wife and kept the Sabbath and Jewish food laws at home for hygienic reasons.</p><p>After two unsuccessful general elections he was elected for the Liberals at the Cleveland by-election of November 1902. In 1909 Prime Minister H. H. Asquith brought him into the Cabinet, and he served as Postmaster General, President of the Local Government Board and Home Secretary. He resigned in December 1916 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> replaced Asquith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In January 1915 Samuel circulated to his Cabinet colleagues a memorandum, The Future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, proposing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> take Palestine to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> and make it a home for the Jewish people. The final version of March 1915 ruled out an immediate Jewish state and insisted on equal treatment for non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. His ideas influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a>. On 23 October 1918 he moved the motion that allowed women to stand for Parliament, which passed by 274 votes to 25.</p><p>Appointed High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in 1920, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> had approved the mandate, he served until 1925. Zionists welcomed the appointment, while the military government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-allenby">Edmund Allenby</a> and Louis Bols called it highly dangerous. Samuel tried to mediate, slowing Jewish immigration and seeking Arab participation, but refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> any power to stop immigration or land purchase. He recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as one of three official languages and chose Haj Amin al-Husseini as Grand Mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Samuel returned to the Commons in 1929 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> from 1931 to 1935, serving as Home Secretary in the National Government from 1931 to 1932. The Liberal ministers resigned in October 1932 over tariffs, and in November 1933 he and most Liberal MPs crossed the floor to oppose the government. Seats fell from 59 to 21 under his leadership. He became Viscount Samuel in 1937 and led the Liberals in the House of Lords from 1944 to 1955. He died on 5 February 1963. Neither Zionist historians nor Arab nationalists remember his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> years kindly, a judgement the historian Wasserstein finds mistaken on both sides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herbert Samuel, 1st Viscount Samuel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1920 a civilian administration under a High Commissioner replaced the military one; the first, Sir <b>Herbert Samuel</b>, a Zionist and recent British cabinet minister, took office on 1 July.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>OETA South was replaced by a civil administration under <b>Herbert Samuel</b> in July 1920, forming Mandatory Palestine, while France occupied inland Syria after defeating the Arab Kingdom of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-enemy-territory-administration">Occupied Enemy Territory Administration</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Liverpool on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4483 327 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Herbert Samuel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4933 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4933 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
